The attached image (Fig. 11) presents histological sections of mouse injection sites, stained with H&E, two weeks after immunization with either aluminum hydroxide (AH) or Nano-11 adjuvants. The left panel, corresponding to AH, shows extensive granulomatous inflammation (asterisks) characterized by dense aggregates of macrophages and scattered eosinophils within the skeletal muscle (M). In contrast, the right panel, representing Nano-11, reveals significantly less residual inflammation, with only relatively few macrophages (asterisk) arranged in slender cords between muscle fibers (M). This indicates that Nano-11 induces a more transient and quickly resolving inflammatory response compared to AH.